# Module — ignore vendor/

`vendor/` is Composer output (third-party packages). It is listed in `.gitignore` and must stay out of module work.

## Do not

- Edit, refactor, or "fix" any file under `vendor/`.
- Run codemods, bulk replace scripts, or SQL/escape passes across `vendor/`.
- Stage, commit, or describe `vendor/` changes in changelog entries or PR summaries.
- Use `vendor/` hits to justify changes in first-party code unless the task is explicitly about a dependency upgrade.

## If vendor appears modified

- Treat it as accidental noise (e.g. a script ran too broadly).
- Do **not** try to "complete" or normalize those edits.
- Restore with `git checkout -- vendor` (or leave untouched and work only under module paths).
- When searching the codebase, exclude `vendor/` (e.g. restrict globs to `classes/`, `functions/`, `controllers/`, `includes/`, `upgrade/`, `cdiscount.php`, etc.).

## First-party scope

Apply fixes and reviews only to module-owned paths: `cdiscount.php`, `classes/`, `controllers/`, `functions/`, `includes/`, `upgrade/`, `views/`, `translations/`, `tools/` (module tools, not Composer), and related project files — never `vendor/`.
